    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **36 tests, 0 failures**, including the new `Cp26Tests`.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe E.164 validation lived in `TelephoneE164.isValid(...)`, called by `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` which raises `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 400). Previously it only enforced the generic \"8\u201315 digits after the `+`\" rule.\n\nI extended `isValid` to also check the national-number length for country codes with a known fixed length, while keeping the generic 8\u201315 bound for everything else:\n\n- `+61` \u2192 national number must be exactly **9** digits (`digits.length() - 2 == 9`)\n- `+1` \u2192 national number must be exactly **10** digits (`digits.length() - 1 == 10`)\n- other country codes (e.g. the `+64` case in `Cp08`) \u2192 fall through to the generic rule\n\nThe `+61` check is ordered before the `+1` check since `+61` also starts with `1`'s prefix digit \u2014 testing `\"61\"` first avoids misclassifying Australian numbers as North American.\n\nVerified against the tests: `+61 123` (3 national digits) is now rejected with 400, while the existing valid cases (`0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, 9 national digits; `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`) still pass.",
